This is the whole `Program.cs` that starts the coding agent in the terminal. It is the only code the program needs. The one line that matters is the call to `CliHost.RunAsync`.

```csharp
using LouisAgent.Core.Web;
using LouisAgent.Core.Anthropic;
using LouisAgent.Core.Ollama;
using LouisAgent.Core.OpenAi;
using LouisAgent.Core.Telemetry;
using LouisAgent.Core.Hosting;
using LouisAgent.Cli;
using LouisAgent.Coding;

// Usage: LouisAgent.CodingCli [prompt].
// It loads what this demo's catalogue names.
await CliHost.RunAsync(args, CodingCatalog.Create(),
    new HostServices(WebAccess.Create, OtlpTelemetry.Instance,
        Providers: [AnthropicProvider.Instance, OllamaProvider.Instance, OpenAiCompatibleProvider.Instance]));
```

To run it, put these settings in the config folder. Use your own key in place of the placeholder.

```
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your-key-here
LLM_PROFILE=ollama
```

The key goes in `.env.secrets`. `LLM_PROFILE=ollama` is optional and picks a local model instead. Then run `dotnet run` from `demos/louis-agent/hosts/LouisAgent.CodingCli`.

Your own agent needs one change. `CodingCatalog.Create()` names the coding agent's identities and packs. To start your own agent, you write your own catalogue and pass it in place of that call. The host and its services stay as they are. The guide on identities covers how a catalogue names them.

